# Data Leak Risk Assessment

## Overview

This project analyzes a data leak incident involving the accidental exposure of internal company documents. The assessment focuses on the principle of least privilege and evaluates how improper access management contributed to the incident. Using guidance from NIST SP 800-53 AC-6, recommendations were developed to reduce the likelihood of similar events occurring in the future.

## Scenario

A sales manager shared an internal folder with their team that contained confidential information, including details about an unreleased product. After the meeting, access to the folder was not revoked. Later, a team member accidentally shared the internal folder with an external business partner instead of the intended promotional materials. The business partner then publicly distributed the link, exposing sensitive company information. 【1-d7a502】

## Objectives

- Identify the factors that contributed to the information leak
- Review the NIST SP 800-53 AC-6 (Least Privilege) control
- Evaluate weaknesses in access management practices
- Recommend improvements to reduce future risk
- Justify security controls using industry frameworks

## Skills Demonstrated

- Risk assessment
- Incident analysis
- Access control review
- Least privilege implementation
- NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF)
- NIST SP 800-53 compliance analysis
- Security documentation

## Key Findings

The primary cause of the incident was excessive access permissions that were not removed after they were no longer required. Employees retained access to sensitive internal resources beyond their business need, increasing the risk of accidental disclosure. 【1-d7a502】

## Recommendations

- Implement automatic access expiration for temporary permissions.
- Perform regular privilege and access reviews.
- Restrict access to sensitive information based on user roles.
- Improve employee awareness regarding data handling procedures.
- Maintain logs of privilege assignments and changes. 【1-d7a502】

